equire us actively to do it are you  hoping by using words like in basilic  very strong language that people will  listen that it won't just get lost in  the commentary of what's going on that  word was attached to the slogan and it's  a slogan's been used by people of all  um  political stripes in the u.s you know  forever wars and the reason i i don't  agree with the slogan is that it first  of all it's not that you've got a you're  not in afghanistan in the same way that  you were 10 or 20 years ago you know we  had a manageable situation we could have  carried on managing it and helping the  country but it's also because  in the end and as i say i say this  despite i've got a huge respect for joe  biden as a as a president and as a  person  a policy  can't be decided by slogan it's got to  be decided on the basis of a strategy  and that's a different thing  and that's really the purpose of what i  was saying you've got to  you know your political slogans are one  thing for campaigns but when you're  looking  at the long-term interests of your  country when you've got a position of  leadership in the world it's important  that you you  you take a step back and you really  you really analyze deeply  what a what is in the interest not just  of your  country and its allies but in the  interest of your own values i just want  to express finally you're obviously in  touch with people in afghanistan do they  do they get a sense that people actually  care or do they think they like you said  they can't rely on people anymore and  they're they're going to be forgotten  and left to it with the taliban  suppressing women's rights i mean that's  a kind way of putting it children being  denied education and anyone who goes  against them is is murdered  i think people in afghanistan  you know they've been through so much in  the last decades  you remember we had the i mean one of  the really sad things about afghanistan  is if you get  some old pictures out from the 1960s  of afghanistan  you'll see it was a country that  had a real future  a moderate country  and  unfortunately they've had literally  almost  half a century  of of of difficulty and and  you know hardship but there is a  there is a group of young people today  who've tasted some of the freedom in the  last 20 years  and i think they will  they will they will find a way  to to reassert themselves and i think  what we've got to  to decide now is  and we should do this on a coordinated  basis with the key countries coming  together is what can we do to help and  support them  and yes of course a lot of afghans at  the moment will feel that they've been  betrayed but i think  it's possible for us to show in time  because we do have some leverage in this  situation  that we still  have a sense of obligation to them and  to our own strategic security interests  and can help and we should  go back 20 years would you do it again  well i've no doubt at the time it was  the right thing  to do because if if you have a  a terrorist attack killing three and a  half thousand innocent people  in in the united states of america and  it comes out of a country run by an  extremist government harboring the  people who  carried out that terrorist attack and  would carry out then more terrorist  attacks if you allow them to carry on  doing so  look we we felt at the time and i  believe rightly that there was no  alternative but to say to the taliban  government at the time you either give  up the al-qaeda leadership or we're  going to remove you from government  so  the point is  this could have turned out differently  and it should have um and i'm you know i  don't  as i'm not  criticizing  people in a in a foolish way i know how  difficult it is to take these decisions  we made mistakes  um you know a lot of mistakes have been  made but the fact is the country did  have a chance  and it was really our own internal  short-term politics  um in the west that has driven in the  last couple of 